{
  "id": "67d4e83c-4c4d-4434-b56a-2ed50945317e",
  "page_title": "'Quickstart: Use Azure OpenAI Service with the Java SDK to generate images'",
  "page_description": "Walkthrough on how to get started with Azure OpenAI and make your first image generation call with the Java SDK.",
  "page_date": "08/24/2023",
  "chunk_title": "Install the Java SDK",
  "chunk_content": "Install the Java SDK   This quickstart uses the Gradle dependency manager. You can find the client library and information for other dependency managers on the Maven Central Repository.  Locate *build.gradle.kts* and open it with your preferred IDE or text editor. Then copy in the following build configuration. This configuration defines the project as a Java application whose entry point is the class OpenAIQuickstart. It imports the Azure AI Vision library.  ```kotlin plugins {     java     application } application {      mainClass.set(\"OpenAIQuickstart\") } repositories {     mavenCentral() } dependencies {     implementation(group = \"com.azure\", name = \"azure-ai-openai\", version = \"1.0.0-beta.3\")     implementation(\"org.slf4j:slf4j-simple:1.7.9\") } ```",
